From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shuhei Kadowaki <aviatesk@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:24:46 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Compiler: Reuse limited call results while their caller is in
 progress

When the recursion heuristic limits the inference of a call, its result
is tombstoned and is not cached, since its accuracy depends on the
inference context. So every call to the same method instance infers it
again, together with all of its limited callees. A frame that makes the
call from several sites, or revisits it while iterating over a cycle,
repeats this work for each call, and the work multiplies with each
nested level of limited calls.

This commit makes the caller keep the limited result, together with the
proof of its dependencies, while the caller is in progress. A later call
to the same method instance, made by the caller or by a frame inferred
on its behalf, reuses that result instead of inferring it again. The
inference context below the caller does not change while the caller is
in progress, so inferring the call again would mostly hit the same
limitation. The new caller retains the proof, as the caller of a newly
inferred limited result does. The result is returned through a new
`return_cached_result` method, so that an `AbstractInterpreter` can
restore its own analysis results for it.

The table below is from release builds of Julia with #63634 reverted
to recreate the large recursive cycles, without and with the change
that avoids copying SCC proofs into every consumer ("SCC"). The harness
infers the compiler's own `typeinf` with an `AbstractInterpreter` that
uses aggressive constant propagation; the table shows its time (median
of three runs) and the numbers of frames it infers and of those that
are tombstoned. JET's self check runs the first analysis of its
`test/self_check.jl`.

|              | harness | frames | tombstoned | JET's self check |
|--------------|--------:|-------:|-----------:|-----------------:|
| before       |  48.5 s | 51,918 |     13,625 |            744 s |
| after        |  38.8 s | 47,418 |      8,243 |            209 s |
| before (SCC) |  23.3 s | 51,918 |     13,625 |            122 s |
| after (SCC)  |  21.2 s | 47,418 |      8,243 |             43 s |

The published results of the harness stay the same, except that 49 of
about 22,200 method instances are no longer published.

